<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan"
              testname="Operator Journey - Reprocessor / Exporter (Submit + Withdraw, 100 users)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Performance test -- operator accreditation journey at scale, split by operator
type (Reprocessor / Exporter, per the labelled fixtures on the /operator test
harness page) and by outcome (Submitted / Withdrawn).

Crawled against localhost:3000 2026-08-16. Reuses the exact request sequence
verified working in operator-accreditation-journey.jmx (tonnage -> tonnage
-authority -> tonnage-cya -> business-plan -> business-plan-detail ->
business-plan-cya -> sampling-plan upload/status/results -> submit-declaration
-> submit-confirmation), plus the Exporter-only sections found by crawling the
"Perf Test Exporters Ltd" fixture (org 61001): select-overseas-sites ->
confirm-overseas-sites -> upload-evidence-for-overseas-site (already-accredited
fixture sites, so this is just a straight "continue" through each -- no new
site or evidence has to be added). Withdrawal was crawled by fully submitting
an application, then confirming that GET/POST /accreditation/withdraw
-application/{id} only succeeds on a Submitted application (NotStarted/Started
applications 302 straight back to the landing page, since withdrawal only
makes sense once you have submitted).

Fixture pools (both stub org 61001 "Perf Test Exporters Ltd" for the Exporter
groups and stub org 61002 "Perf Test Reprocessors Ltd" for the Reprocessor
groups) offer 7 materials x 14 years (2024-2037) = 98 slots each; the
landing page gets-or-creates an application keyed on (registrationId,
materialType, year), so every (material, year) CSV row is a genuinely
distinct application. The Submit and Withdraw CSVs for the same org draw from
disjoint (material, year) ranges so they never touch the same application.

Load profile: 100 users total, ramping 2 -> 100 over 5s. Each of the 4 thread
groups starts its first thread at t=0 (4 concurrent immediately -- stock
JMeter has no built-in "start at N" primitive, so 4 is the practical stand-in
for "starts low"), then stages its remaining threads evenly across
ThreadGroup.ramp_time=5s. on_sample_error=continue so one user's failure
doesn't block others sharing a thread group (though each thread only runs a
single iteration here, unlike the case-management plan's worker-pool pattern).

Thread Groups:
  1. Reprocessor -- Submit   (45 threads) org 61002, cms-...-repro-submit.csv
  2. Exporter    -- Submit   (45 threads) org 61001, cms-...-exporter-submit.csv
  3. Reprocessor -- Withdraw ( 5 threads) org 61002, cms-...-repro-withdraw.csv
  4. Exporter    -- Withdraw ( 5 threads) org 61001, cms-...-exporter-withdraw.csv
Total: 100 users / 100 distinct applications, 90 -> Submitted, 10 -> Withdrawn
(withdrawn ones are also fully submitted first, then withdrawn -- there is no
route to withdraw an application that was never submitted).</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.tearDown_on_shutdown">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments"
                   guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(base_url,localhost)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PORT" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PORT</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(port,3000)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PROTOCOL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PROTOCOL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(protocol,http)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="DATA_DIR" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">DATA_DIR</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(data_dir,jmeter/data)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="AUTHORISER_EMAIL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">AUTHORISER_EMAIL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">perf-auth-${__time(yyyyMMddHHmmss)}-${__threadNum}@test.example</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </elementProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <ConfigTestElement guiclass="HttpDefaultsGui" testclass="ConfigTestElement"
                         testname="HTTP Request Defaults" enabled="true">
        <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments">
          <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
        </elementProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${BASE_URL}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${PORT}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">${PROTOCOL}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">10000</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">30000</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.follow_redirects">true</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.auto_redirects">false</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.use_keepalive">true</boolProp>
      </ConfigTestElement>
